  \n- **No Honors** (1 point) if no honors; we have none, so okay.  \n- **Self Drawn** (1 point) if we draw winning tile.  \n\nConsidering tile availability from unshown tiles:  \n- W1: 2 left, W2: 1 left, W4: 1 left (but we have W4 in hand and in meld, so maybe not needed), W6: 1 left, W8: 1 left, T6: 3 left, B1: 2 left.  \n\nWe need to form melds from hand. Current hand pairs: W1 pair (could be pair for winning). Other tiles are singles: W2, W4, W6, W8, T6, B1. We need two more melds or one meld and a pair (if we use W1 as pair). Options:  \n- Keep W1 pair, try to form chows or pungs from other tiles.  \n- Discard a tile that is less useful for forming melds.  \n\nPotential chows:  \n- With W2, need W1 and W3 for Chow 1-2-3, but W3 is in opponent's meld (across has W3-W5), and W1 we have, but W3 unshown: 1 left. Possible but low probability.  \n- With W4, we already have W4 in meld, but could form another Chow with W3-W4-W5 or W4-W5-W6; we have W6, so W4-W5-W6 possible if we get W5 (unshown: 1 left). But we already have W4-W5-W6 meld, so duplicate chow could give Pure Double Chow (1 point) if we form another identical Chow.  \n- With W6, could form Chow W4-W5-W6 (same as above) or W5-W6-W7 (W7 unshown: 4 left) or W6-W7-W8 (we have W8). W6-W7-W8 possible if we get W7 (4 left, good).  \n- With W8, could form Chow W6-W7-W8 (need W7) or W7-W8-W9 (W9 unshown: 1 left).  \n- T6: could form Chow T4-T5-T6 (we have T4, T5 in meld? Actually meld is T3-T4-T5, so T4 and T5 are used, but we could form another Chow with T4-T5-T6 if we get T4 and T5, but T4 unshown: 2 left, T5: 3 left, possible. Or T5-T6-T7 (T7 unshown: 3 left).  \n- B1: could form Chow B1-B2-B3, but B2 unshown: 1 left, B3: 2 left, possible.  \n\nGiven the exposed melds:  \n- Next player has B1-B2-B3 Chow and J1 Kong, so B1-B2-B3 is less likely because one set already taken.  \n- Across has W3-W4-W5, B7-B8-B9, T2-T3-T4.  \n- Previous has B7-B8-B9, B6-B7-B8, B5 pung.  \n\nThis affects tile availability: B tiles are heavily used, especially B5-B9. W tiles: W3-W5 used, W4-W6 used in our meld. T tiles: T2-T4 used, T3-T5 used in our meld.  \n\nThus, the most promising suit is W because we have many W tiles and potential chows with W7 (4 left) and W6/W8. Also, keeping W tiles may help Half Flush if we discard B1 and T6? But T6 is part of T suit, and Half Flush allows one suit + honors, so we need to choose either W or T as main suit. Our melds are W and T, so we could aim for Half Flush with W suit only, meaning we need to discard all T and B tiles. But we have T6 in hand and T meld already exposed, so Half Flush is still possible if we consider the exposed melds as part of the hand? In scoring, exposed melds count toward patterns, so Half Flush requires that all tiles (hand and melds) are only from one suit and honors. Currently, our melds are W and T suits, so we have two suits, breaking Half Flush. Therefore, Half Flush is impossible because we already have both W and T melds exposed. So discard Half Flush idea.  \n\nSimilarly, All Simples is possible if we avoid terminals and honors; W1 is terminal, so discarding W1 helps All Simples. But we have a pair of W1, which could be useful as the winning pair.  \n\nScoring potential: We need at least 8 points to win. Possible patterns from current hand:  \n- All Simples (2 points)  \n- One Voided Suit (2 points) if we void one suit (discard B1 to void B, but we still have T and W in hand and melds, so that's two suits, so One Voided Suit counts if exactly two suits are present? Pattern description: \"A hand with exactly two of the three suits of tiles.\" So if we have only W and T suits in the entire hand (including melds), that qualifies. Currently, we have W and T in melds, and if we discard B1, our hand has only W and T tiles, so overall we have exactly two suits (W and T), achieving One Voided Suit (2 points). But wait, we also have B1 in hand; if we keep B1, we have three suits, so no. So discarding B1 gives One Voided Suit.  \n- No Honors (1 point)  \n- All Chows (2 points) if we complete two more chows (possible).  \n- Self Drawn (1 point) if we draw winning tile.  \n- Edge Wait/Closed Wait/Single Wait (1 point each) depending on wait.  \n- Pure Double Chow (1 point) if we form another identical Chow to existing ones.  \n- Short Straight (1 point) if we have two chows in same suit with successive numbers. We have W4-W5-W6 and could form W6-W7-W8, which is successive? Actually W4-W5-W6 and W6-W7-W8 share W6, but they are not successive numbers in sequence; Short Straight is like 234 and 567, so two chows that are consecutive in numbers without overlap. We don't have that yet.  \n\nSo total points from small patterns might not reach 8 unless we get higher-value patterns. We may need to aim for patterns like Full Flush (24 points) but that requires all tiles same suit, impossible with current melds. Or All Pungs (6 points) but we have chows.  \n\nGiven the need to maximize flexibility and potential for higher points, we should discard a tile that is less useful and keeps the hand versatile.  \n\nDiscard candidates:  \n- **B1**: Discarding B1 voids B suit, giving One Voided Suit (2 points), and removes a tile from a suit that is heavily used by opponents (many B melds). B1 is isolated, only chow potential with B2-B3, but B2 and B3 are scarce (1 and 2 left). Good discard.  \n- **W1**: Discarding W1 keeps All Simples potential, but loses a pair. Having a pair is important for winning. If we discard one W1, we have single W1, which is not useful for pair. We could form a Chow with W1-W2-W3, but W3 is scarce (1 left) and used in opponent meld. So W1 is not great for chows. But keeping W1 pair gives us a ready pair for winning. So maybe keep W1.  \n- **W2**: W2 can be used in Chow W1-W2-W3 or W2-W3-W4. W3 is scarce, W4 we have. Not great.  \n- **W4**: We already have W4 in meld, but could form another Chow with W4-W5-W6 or W3-W4-W5. But duplicate chow gives Pure Double Chow (1 point). Keeping W4 might be okay.  \n- **W6**: Useful for Chow with W4-W5-W6 or W5-W6-W7 or W6-W7-W8. W7 is abundant (4 left). Good keeper.  \n- **W8**: Useful for Chow W6-W7-W8 (W7 abundant) or W7-W8-W9. Good keeper.  \n- **T6**: Useful for Chow with T4-T5-T6 or T5-T6-T7. T5 and T7 are moderately available.  \n\nThus, the least useful tiles are B1 and maybe W2. Discarding B1 seems best because it opens One Voided Suit and removes a suit.  \n\nAlso, consider game history: Previous player has played many B tiles and made B melds, so B tiles might be safer to discard (less likely to give others pung/kong).  \n\nFinal choice: Discard B1.  \n\nReason: Discarding B1 achieves One Voided Suit (2 points), removes a suit that is overrepresented in opponents' melds, and keeps the hand flexible with W tiles that have good potential for chows using abundant W7. Keeping the W1 pair provides a ready pair for winning.  \n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
